Answer: Since $T$ is sufficient for $\theta$, the conditional expectation of $T_1$ given $T=t$ is independent of $\theta$. Thus $h(t)$ is independent of $\theta$. Using the result of conditional expectation, we see that
\begin{equation*}
E(T_1)=E[E(T_1\mid T)]=E[h(T)] \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
which shows that $T_1$ and $h(T)$ have the same expectation. Furthermore,
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
E\{h(T)[T_1-h(T)]\}
&=E\{h(T)[E(T_1-h(T)\mid T)]\} \\
&=0,\quad\text{since }E(T_1\mid T=t)=h(t).
\end{aligned}\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Consider
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
E[T_1-g(\theta)]^2
&=E[T_1-h(T)+h(T)-g(\theta)]^2 \\
&=E[T_1-h(T)]^2+E[h(T)-g(\theta)]^2 \\
&\geq E[h(T)-g(\theta)]^2,
\end{aligned}\tag{3}
\end{equation*}
since the product term
\begin{align*}
E[T_1-h(T)][h(T)-g(\theta)]
&=E\{[T_1-h(T)]h(T)\}-g(\theta)E[T_1-h(T)]\\
&=0-0=0,
\end{align*}
if we use (1) and (2). The equality in (3) is attained only when $T_1=h(T)$. From (1), $E[h(T)]=g(\theta)$ if $E(T_1)=g(\theta)$.

